@*
    =================================================================================================
     App.razor  —  the root component: the ONE real HTML document of the whole web app
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     What it is:
       Program.cs calls  app.MapRazorComponents<App>(),  so every browser request for a page renders
       this component first.  It produces <html>, <head> and <body>; everything else (layout, pages)
       is rendered INSIDE it by the <Routes> component.

     What the student sees on screen:
       Nothing of its own; it only provides the frame (stylesheet, Blazor script, theme helper).

     How Blazor works here (Interactive Server render mode):
       1. The first request returns normal, pre-rendered HTML (fast first paint).
       2. blazor.web.js (loaded below) then opens a SignalR connection (a WebSocket) to the server.
          That connection is called a "circuit".  The components keep living ON THE SERVER, one set
          per browser tab.
       3. A click in the browser travels over the circuit to the server, C# runs there, and the
          server sends back only the changed parts of the HTML ("render diff").  The server can also
          push a re-render on its own, e.g. when a new message was projected into the read model.
       So the browser never calls our APIs, never queries a database and never talks to RabbitMQ.

     Data source: none (pages below this one read the Web's read model).

     Read next: Routes.razor, then Layout/MainLayout.razor.
    =================================================================================================
*@
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    @* <base href="/"> makes relative URLs (app.css, _framework/...) resolve from the site root on every page. *@
    <base href="/" />
    @* Our only stylesheet: wwwroot/app.css (light + dark theme, layout, developer console). *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="app.css" />
    @* HeadOutlet renders whatever pages put into <PageTitle>/<HeadContent>.  It needs the same
       interactive render mode as the router so the browser tab title updates while navigating. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveServer" />
</head>
<body>
    @* The router, made interactive for the whole app: every page below runs in Interactive Server mode
       (no page needs its own @rendermode).  This is a "global" interactivity setting. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveServer" />
    @* The Blazor runtime script: starts the SignalR circuit and applies the HTML diffs the server sends. *@
    <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
    @* Three small JavaScript helpers, called from C# through IJSRuntime (see MainLayout and the
       developer components):
         miniShop.copy(text)      — copy an id to the clipboard ("copy" buttons)
         miniShop.setTheme(theme) — switch light/dark: sets <html data-theme="..."> which app.css reads,
                                    and remembers the choice in localStorage
         miniShop.getTheme()      — read the remembered theme (defaults to light)
       The try/catch blocks protect against browsers where localStorage is blocked.
       The last line applies the saved theme immediately, before Blazor starts, so the page does not
       flash in the wrong colours. *@
    <script>
        // Tiny helpers used by the Developer Console (copy ids, remember the theme).
        window.miniShop = {
            copy: text => navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText(text),
            setTheme: theme => { document.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme; try { localStorage.setItem('minishop-theme', theme); } catch { } },
            getTheme: () => { try { return localStorage.getItem('minishop-theme') || 'light'; } catch { return 'light'; } }
        };
        document.documentElement.dataset.theme = window.miniShop.getTheme();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
